# WITNESS for HB_WSB (215) -- the ATOMIC (pointer-free) collected-heap byte buffer.
# &date is served by kw_read (keywords.c), which formats its result into a buffer from rt_wsb_alloc.
# An HB_WSB block holds no pointers by construction, so the collector never scans its interior -- but it is
# still MARKED and still RELOCATED like every other block, and the reference to it must still be fixed up when
# it slides. This witness proves that distinction is the right one rather than a licence to ignore the block:
# if the root is lost, or found and not fixed up, the read returns 0xDB poison instead of the bytes.
# Positions 5 and 8 of &date are the yyyy/mm/dd separators, so the expectation is stable on any day.
procedure main();
   local held, i, churn;
   held := &date;
   every i := 1 to 300 do churn := repl("z", 100);
   write(*held, "-", held[5], held[8]);
end
